# G122 — Wallpaper Manager

*v2. See `wallpaper-manager.md` for prose: multi-monitor slot assignment,
asymmetric resolution fit score, recency filter with fallback, schedule
policy ADT, monotonic-to-circular time mapping.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Workspace Theming

[wallpaper-theming @monitors @wallpapers @schedule
    IN ORDER TO
        rotate wallpapers across all attached monitors independently
        matching each monitor's resolution to the best variant
        and honoring a schedule that varies by time of day

    @mgr <- [wm[new-manager {schedule: @schedule,
                              recency-window: 3,
                              seed: 42}]]

    for @m in @monitors
        [wm[add-monitor {manager: @mgr, monitor: @m}]]
    for @w in @wallpapers
        [wm[add-wallpaper {manager: @mgr, wallpaper: @w}]]

    until @shutdown [
        at @every 1000 ms
            [wm[tick {manager: @mgr, ms: 1000}]]

            for @m in @monitors
                @variant <- [wm[current-variant-for-monitor
                                  {manager: @mgr, monitor-id: @m:id}]]
                    || -> #warn "no variant for monitor " + @m:id
                [wayland[set-wallpaper {monitor: @m, path: @variant:path}]]
    ]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[wm
    new-manager                   {schedule, recency-window, seed}
    add-monitor                   {manager, monitor}
    add-wallpaper                 {manager, wallpaper}
    tick                          {manager, ms}
    rotate-monitor                {manager, monitor-id, tag-filter?}
    current-variant-for-monitor   {manager, monitor-id}
    resolution-fit-score          {monitor-res, variant-res}
    best-variant-for-monitor      {wallpaper, monitor-res}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: schedule as a named choreography

The v1 modeled `Schedule::{Interval, FixedList, TagWindow}` as a
discriminated union. In Innate, each schedule kind is its own
named choreography the manager references:

[interval-every @ms
    recurrence: @every @ms
]

[fixed-list @times
    recurrence: @at @each @time in @times
]

[tag-window @tag @start @end @inner-ms
    at @every @inner-ms
    where time-of-day in [@start, @end)
    tag-filter: @tag
]

A wallpaper manager takes `@schedule` — whichever choreography the
user named — and dispatches against its recurrence shape. The
manager doesn't care which schedule it received; the recurrence
operator handles all three.

---

## Resolution fit as `where`-expressed scoring

[wm[best-variant-for-monitor {wallpaper: @w, monitor-res: @m}]
    where
        exact match scores 1000
        and aspect-ratio match scores up to 500
            with 0 aspect difference scoring 500
            and 0.5 aspect difference scoring 0
        and pixel-count proximity scores up to 300
            with upscaling penalized more than downscaling
            (a 1080p monitor prefers the 4K variant over the 720p variant)
    ordered by score descending
    first
]

The scoring contract is documented in the `where` clauses; the
resolver implements whichever formula satisfies them.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @mgr <- [wm[new-manager {schedule: [interval-every 1000],
                              recency-window: 2, seed: 42}]]

    [wm[add-monitor {manager: @mgr, monitor:
                      {id: 1, resolution: {w: 1920, h: 1080}}}]]
    [wm[add-monitor {manager: @mgr, monitor:
                      {id: 2, resolution: {w: 2560, h: 1440}}}]]

    for @id in [10, 11, 12, 13, 14]
        [wm[add-wallpaper {manager: @mgr,
                            wallpaper: {id: @id,
                                         variants: [{res: {w: 1920, h: 1080}}
                                                     {res: {w: 2560, h: 1440}}]}}]]

    [wm[tick {manager: @mgr, ms: 1}]]
    -> @mgr:assignments[1]:current-wallpaper-id     ;; -> 13
    -> @mgr:assignments[2]:current-wallpaper-id     ;; -> 13

    [wm[tick {manager: @mgr, ms: 1100}]]
    -> @mgr:assignments[1]:current-wallpaper-id     ;; -> 10 (13 in recency)
    -> @mgr:assignments[2]:current-wallpaper-id     ;; -> 14
]
